  data:  # 816b
    kind: work_item  # 816b
    title: "DECISION: the taint model and every precision rubric to date cannot separate a value that LEAKS from one a config-driven program was TOLD to act on -- every configured path is a true positive"  # 816b
    status: todo_hard  # 816b
    priority: 2  # 816b
    parent:  # 816b
    tags:  # 816b
    - taint  # 816b
    - adr_0017  # 816b
    - analysis_quality  # 816b
    - measurement  # 816b
    isbefore: []  # 816b
    duplicate_of: []  # 816b
    not_duplicate_of: []  # 816b
    pr_ref:  # 816b
    description: "Measurement 0005's adjudication surfaced a limit that is not a bug in any producer and not a precision miss: the taint model, and the rubric every precision measurement of it has used, cannot distinguish a value that LEAKS from a value that a config-driven program was TOLD to act on.\n\nTHE CONCRETE CASE. caddy reads its config from stdin (`caddy run --config -`). That config names a log file. The bytes therefore flow: cmd/main.go:164 io.ReadAll(os.Stdin) -> caddy.Load -> changeConfig -> unsyncedDecodeAndRun -> StrictUnmarshalJSON -> BaseLog.WriterRaw -> ctx.LoadModule(cl,\"WriterRaw\") -> FileWriter.Filename -> filewriter.go:239 os.Chmod(fw.Filename, configuredMode). Every hop is citable in caddy's own source; the module constructor is pinned by RegisterModule + a literal module ID, and the field write by a json struct tag. Under the 0001 rubric -- TP is data flow into a sink argument, and \"exploitability is not the question\" -- this is a TRUE POSITIVE, and an independent adjudication commissioned for this exact question said so and accepted the consequence in terms: under this rubric EVERY filesystem, network and subprocess operation whose argument comes from a configured value is a true positive from the config source, for any config-driven server.\n\nWHY THAT MATTERS RATHER THAN BEING A CURIOSITY. It is TRUE and UNINFORMATIVE at the same time. caddy's config IS a program for the filesystem, so \"untrusted input reached os.MkdirAll\" is a correct sentence that tells a reader nothing they did not already know from running the program. Uninformativeness is an actionability/severity axis; precision is a correctness axis; the rubric deliberately admits only the second. So the metric cannot see this class at all, and the class is not small -- it is every config field of every module in a plugin-registry architecture.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR MEASUREMENT. 0005's row rate is 10.6%; without these five rows it is 7.9%. A future measurement on a config-heavy repo could be dominated by them, and would report a precision IMPROVEMENT that is entirely an artefact of how much config the repo has.\n\nTWO CANDIDATE REMEDIES, neither costed yet:\n(a) A FOURTH ADJUDICATION CATEGORY -- e.g. \"config-intended effect\" -- reported beside TP/FP/UNADJ the way UNADJ already is, never folded into precision. Cheap, honest, and confined to the measurement instrument; it changes no producer and no verdict a user sees.\n(b) A BARRIER AT THE CONFIG-DESERIALIZATION BOUNDARY in the taint model itself -- treat a value that has passed through a declared configuration decode as no longer carrying its original boundary label, or as carrying a distinct one. Far more invasive, changes what users are shown, and needs its own precision measurement in both directions before anyone believes it.\n\nFILED, NOT CHASED. This is not a defect to fix under the current campaign; it is a named limit on what the precision metric can mean, and the owner should choose (a), (b) or neither before the next measurement is designed. Evidence: docs/measurements/0005, the adjudication record in the run directory (blind/adjudication-unadj.json)."  # 816b
    fields: {}  # 816b
